/T (Model of fusible links for use in a cylindrical cell battery pack)
/E (Model of fusible links for use in a cylindrical cell battery pack)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
148 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H6
/P 131 0 R
/T (Fuses in a battery pack isolate faulty cells and prevent dangerous current surges. In cylindrical-cell packs, spot-welded nickel strips often serve double duty as both cell interconnects and fusible links, so their geometry has to be tuned to fuse at a target fault current without failing during normal operation.)
/E (Fuses in a battery pack isolate faulty cells and prevent dangerous current surges. In cylindrical-cell packs, spot-welded nickel strips often serve double duty as both cell interconnects and fusible links, so their geometry has to be tuned to fuse at a target fault current without failing during normal operation.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
149 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H6
/P 131 0 R
/T (This project set out to determine what nickel strip geometry fuses reliably at a specified current. To evaluate candidate geometries, I modeled cell temperature over time in Ansys for a given current and strip design, using Joule heating to predict whether and when the link reaches its fusing point.)
/E (This project set out to determine what nickel strip geometry fuses reliably at a specified current. To evaluate candidate geometries, I modeled cell temperature over time in Ansys for a given current and strip design, using Joule heating to predict whether and when the link reaches its fusing point.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
150 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H6
/P 131 0 R
/T (I used a coupled transient approach in Ansys Mechanical, combining electrothermal analysis for heat generation with thermal analysis for temperature rise. I defined nickel's resistivity as a temperature-dependent curve, so at each time step the solver computes Joule heating from the strip's current resistance, updates the temperature, then reads the new resistance off the curve before the next step. )
/E (I used a coupled transient approach in Ansys Mechanical, combining electrothermal analysis for heat generation with thermal analysis for temperature rise. I defined nickel's resistivity as a temperature-dependent curve, so at each time step the solver computes Joule heating from the strip's current resistance, updates the temperature, then reads the new resistance off the curve before the next step. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
151 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H6
/P 131 0 R
/T (This two-way coupling matters because nickel's resistivity climbs with temperature: as the strip heats, it generates more heat per amp, which accelerates the run toward the fusing point. Iterating over the simulation period captures the strip's full temperature-versus-time response, including this feedback behavior near failure.)
/E (This two-way coupling matters because nickel's resistivity climbs with temperature: as the strip heats, it generates more heat per amp, which accelerates the run toward the fusing point. Iterating over the simulation period captures the strip's full temperature-versus-time response, including this feedback behavior near failure.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
