720 0 obj
<<
/Title (Fuses in a battery pack isolate faulty cells and prevent dangerous current surges. In cylindrical-cell packs, spot-welded nickel strips often serve double duty as both cell interconnects and fusible links, so their geometry has to be tuned to fuse at a target fault current without failing during normal operation.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 626 0 R
/Next 1338 0 R
>>
endobj
721 0 obj
<<
/Title (This two-way coupling matters because nickel's resistivity climbs with temperature: as the strip heats, it generates more heat per amp, which accelerates the run toward the fusing point. Iterating over the simulation period captures the strip's full temperature-versus-time response, including this feedback behavior near failure.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 626 0 R
/Prev 1339 0 R
>>
endobj
722 0 obj
<<
/Title (The current model treats the nickel strip in isolation, which makes it a conservative baseline. Planned extensions include modeling conductive heat transfer into the connected cells, which act as heat sinks that would raise the true fusing current; simulating dynamic current profiles rather than a fixed current, to reflect real fault and load conditions; and modeling more complex strip geometries beyond the simplified shapes used here.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 627 0 R
/Next 723 0 R
>>
endobj
723 0 obj
<<
/Title (My Role: Full design and implementation \(individual Project\) Software Used: Ansys Mechanical and Ansys Workbench)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 627 0 R
/Prev 722 0 R
>>
endobj
724 0 obj
<<
/Title (The objective of the simulation is to predict cell temperature and state of charge across a full endurance run for a theoretical Molicel P60C pack.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 628 0 R
/Next 725 0 R
>>
endobj
725 0 obj
<<
/Title (Using a real LapSim current profile and SOC-dependent DCIR from Molicel's published data, the model computes ohmic heat generation and temperature rise over time to determine whether the pack requires active cooling.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 628 0 R
/Prev 724 0 R
>>
endobj
