1338 0 obj
<<
/Title (This project set out to determine what nickel strip geometry fuses reliably at a specified current. To evaluate candidate geometries, I modeled cell temperature over time in Ansys for a given current and strip design, using Joule heating to predict whether and when the link reaches its fusing point.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 626 0 R
/Prev 720 0 R
/Next 1339 0 R
>>
endobj
1339 0 obj
<<
/Title (I used a coupled transient approach in Ansys Mechanical, combining electrothermal analysis for heat generation with thermal analysis for temperature rise. I defined nickel's resistivity as a temperature-dependent curve, so at each time step the solver computes Joule heating from the strip's current resistance, updates the temperature, then reads the new resistance off the curve before the next step.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 626 0 R
/Prev 1338 0 R
/Next 721 0 R
>>
endobj
1340 0 obj
<<
/Title (The model is quite limited. The cell is treated as a single lumped thermal mass; heat generation is ohmic only, omitting entropic heat; current is derived from power using a fixed nominal voltage, ignoring voltage sag; and DCIR is a function of SOC only, without temperature dependence. The convective term and material properties are typical Li-ion assumptions rather than measured values.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 726 0 R
/Next 1341 0 R
>>
endobj
1341 0 obj
<<
/Title (Its strength is how little it needs. From only a DCIR curve, it produces useful data before a physical cell exists, or is even released, making it a practical screening tool for comparing candidate cells ahead of any bench testing. Measuring DCIR and temperature rise on a real cell to calibrate hA and confirm the model is the next step.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 726 0 R
/Prev 1340 0 R
>>
endobj
1342 0 obj
<<
/Title (The objective of the Benchtop PDB is to facilitate power connections and testing of custom PCBs in a bench environment. It features individually toggleable power outputs, a 5V output, and a 3.3V output.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 729 0 R
/Next 1363 0 R
>>
endobj
1343 0 obj
<<
/Title (Technical Specifications)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 729 0 R
/Prev 1364 0 R
/First 1365 0 R
/Last 1366 0 R
/Count -9
>>
endobj
1344 0 obj
<<
/Title (Each output channel is individually fused, and a 5A fuse also protects the main power input. The design includes reverse-polarity protection via a P-channel MOSFET \(NTTFS5116PLTWG\) that blocks current if reverse voltage is applied, avoiding the forward voltage drop penalty of a series diode solution. Transient-voltage protection is implemented with a TVS diode that clamps at 40V to safeguard connected hardware from input voltage spikes.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 730 0 R
/First 1367 0 R
/Last 1368 0 R
/Count -2
>>
endobj
